// Sub-plan D fixture: the width solver reaches a fixpoint over backward
// edges.
//
// A backward branch is an edge from later in the instruction stream to
// earlier, so a loop head can be reached at one width on the first arrival
// and another on the back edge. A single forward traversal accepts
// `agreeing` and silently mis-sizes `diverging`, which is why this file
// exists: the two differ only in the direction of the mode change inside the
// loop.

// The head is entered at @a8 and closed from @a16, so no width holds there.
// The `lda` is rejected rather than sized against either arrival.
func diverging @a8 {
.head:
    lda #$01
    rep #$20
    bra .head
}

// The back edge agrees with the entry contract, so the head stays 8-bit and
// the same `lda` is accepted.
func agreeing @a8 {
.head:
    lda #$01
    sep #$20
    bra .head
}
